(363187, 27884, 6611, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by emorgan49:</b></div><hr/><i>The border competes with the image.</i></td></tr></table> <br/>Thanks. I understand my mistake.</td>', 'Originally posted by emorgan49:The border competes with the image. Thanks. I understand my mistake.', '2003-07-22 04:07:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(363200, 28001, 9073, '<td valign="top">*critique club*<br/>Overall<br/>This photo meets the challenge well. You''ve captured a romantic night on the town. I wonder if you''re making a pun, too - a Knight with steed and wench in tow? Certainly, the imagination is allowed to run loose here. This is in stark contrast to the quite rigid poses of the models, who appear relaxed enough but somewhat off balance. Usually, it''s better to have models look at the camera in a posed shot. Here, ironically, it''s the human who''s looking away.<br/> <br/>Composition<br/>I don''t like the white pole extruding from the human''s head. It seems to accentuate the straightness of her body negatively. Also, being the brightest thing in the shot, it draws the eyes away from the key elements. The human is bang in the middle but looking through to the left. The horse is on the right but looking directly ahead. There seems to be an unbalance of objects, then. The horse''s direct gaze calls for more space to work within. With the woman looking expectantly at something outside the photo and the horse at the camera, I feel rather that the photo loses a lot of its impact. The bright spots in the eyes also reinforce this imbalance. <br/> <br/>Your f-stop is wide, but the carriage is clear, meaning that the dof is deeper than I think you had imagined. Certainly, the camera angle downplays any import for the carriage in this shot. Being out of focus would have helped your composition somewhat. If you wanted a sharp carriage, maybe you should have taken a shot further from the left. That might have pulled the inquisitive horse''s eyes round to match the woman''s, too. While doing so, you might correct the horizon, too. <br/> <br/>Colour<br/>I find the overall colour range too much on the dark side. Checking with Photoshop, I found that (except the pure white pole) most of the important body parts, eyes, face, teeth, even the arms, were in the 17% - 70% grey range. With b&amp;w, you really need to push the tonal boundaries more, especially with potentially dark shots like this. Find the point which you can accept as being the brightest, then work backwards from there. (The white spots in the horse''s eye are 100% white, a feature totally missing in the girl, which accounts for some of the problems there.) <br/> <br/>To sum up, more tonal contrast, more thought about the composition and more consideration about the dof and you''ll have a great shot. As it stands, this one is one worthy of re-doing, don''t you think?<br/> <br/>Best wishes,<br/> <br/>Jim<br/></td>', '*critique club*OverallThis photo meets the challenge well. You''ve captured a romantic night on the town. I wonder if you''re making a pun, too - a Knight with steed and wench in tow? Certainly, the imagination is allowed to run loose here. This is in stark contrast to the quite rigid poses of the models, who appear relaxed enough but somewhat off balance. Usually, it''s better to have models look at the camera in a posed shot. Here, ironically, it''s the human who''s looking away. CompositionI don''t like the white pole extruding from the human''s head. It seems to accentuate the straightness of her body negatively. Also, being the brightest thing in the shot, it draws the eyes away from the key elements. The human is bang in the middle but looking through to the left. The horse is on the right but looking directly ahead. There seems to be an unbalance of objects, then. The horse''s direct gaze calls for more space to work within. With the woman looking expectantly at something outside the photo and the horse at the camera, I feel rather that the photo loses a lot of its impact. The bright spots in the eyes also reinforce this imbalance.  Your f-stop is wide, but the carriage is clear, meaning that the dof is deeper than I think you had imagined. Certainly, the camera angle downplays any import for the carriage in this shot. Being out of focus would have helped your composition somewhat. If you wanted a sharp carriage, maybe you should have taken a shot further from the left. That might have pulled the inquisitive horse''s eyes round to match the woman''s, too. While doing so, you might correct the horizon, too.  ColourI find the overall colour range too much on the dark side. Checking with Photoshop, I found that (except the pure white pole) most of the important body parts, eyes, face, teeth, even the arms, were in the 17% - 70% grey range. With b&w, you really need to push the tonal boundaries more, especially with potentially dark shots like this. Find the point which you can accept as being the brightest, then work backwards from there. (The white spots in the horse''s eye are 100% white, a feature totally missing in the girl, which accounts for some of the problems there.)  To sum up, more tonal contrast, more thought about the composition and more consideration about the dof and you''ll have a great shot. As it stands, this one is one worthy of re-doing, don''t you think? Best wishes, Jim', '2003-07-22 04:36:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(363423, 27801, 6202, '<td valign="top">*Critique Corner*<br/> <br/>It is a very pretty image the table looks so nice.  But it really doesn''t say a lot about a night on the town, it says a formal dinner well though.  <br/> <br/>Your focus is very good but there is a strong glare coming off the plate, and the one crystal goblet appears to blend into the tableclothe.<br/> <br/>Your composition here is very good and there are a number of possibilities here you just need to concentrate a little more on the little minor details and you will have an outstanding photo.<br/> <br/>Anna</td>', '*Critique Corner* It is a very pretty image the table looks so nice.  But it really doesn''t say a lot about a night on the town, it says a formal dinner well though.   Your focus is very good but there is a strong glare coming off the plate, and the one crystal goblet appears to blend into the tableclothe. Your composition here is very good and there are a number of possibilities here you just need to concentrate a little more on the little minor details and you will have an outstanding photo. Anna', '2003-07-22 11:42:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(363429, 27549, 6202, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Very nice image the lighting here is fantastic, the focus is good and your composition here is exceptionally good.  Make sure you pay attention to you center point.  Although the building is centered the angle you took the photo from left you cropping the statues off a different points and makes the image appear a little less polished.<br/> <br/>Overall it does reflect night life so it does meet the challenge.<br/> <br/>Anna</td>', '*Critique Club* Very nice image the lighting here is fantastic, the focus is good and your composition here is exceptionally good.  Make sure you pay attention to you center point.  Although the building is centered the angle you took the photo from left you cropping the statues off a different points and makes the image appear a little less polished. Overall it does reflect night life so it does meet the challenge. Anna', '2003-07-22 11:47:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(363434, 27887, 6202, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>This is one of those adorable photos.  The look on his face is unforgetable.  Your focus here is good and it was a cute idea but your lighting is just a little to dark.<br/> <br/>As cute as it is it has one problem with not really meeting the challenge as you said yourself it was a night to remember but at the same time the photo just doesn''t say night on the town.<br/> <br/>Anna</td>', '*Critique Club* This is one of those adorable photos.  The look on his face is unforgetable.  Your focus here is good and it was a cute idea but your lighting is just a little to dark. As cute as it is it has one problem with not really meeting the challenge as you said yourself it was a night to remember but at the same time the photo just doesn''t say night on the town. Anna', '2003-07-22 11:52:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(363445, 27334, 6202, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>From the moment I saw this photo I had to smile.  The title says so much about the health craze.  It is so amusing to see that sitting out on the street corner.  <br/> <br/>Your lighting here is good, the focus is also good, and you know I liked this from the beginning but now looking at it I wonder what it would have looked like if you had tried to use a shallower dof leaving the lights and buildings in the background more blurred.  <br/> <br/>To bad you didn''t have Isaac with you to work out while you were taking the photo...would have made a much strong statement about night out on the town.  Overall, I think this was a very humorous take on this challenge. Nicely done.<br/> <br/>Anna</td>', '*Critique Club* From the moment I saw this photo I had to smile.  The title says so much about the health craze.  It is so amusing to see that sitting out on the street corner.   Your lighting here is good, the focus is also good, and you know I liked this from the beginning but now looking at it I wonder what it would have looked like if you had tried to use a shallower dof leaving the lights and buildings in the background more blurred.   To bad you didn''t have Isaac with you to work out while you were taking the photo...would have made a much strong statement about night out on the town.  Overall, I think this was a very humorous take on this challenge. Nicely done. Anna', '2003-07-22 12:03:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(363464, 27299, 6202, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>What a silly photo.  As I look at it I have to say it really is good considering all the issues. I''m guessing the spots in the photo were raindrops on your lens.  Your focus isn''t to bad and you have a very cute idea here, the umbrella adds the humor to the photo.  Only real attention getter in this photo is that white bag it seems overly exposed.<br/> <br/>It does meet the challenge well.<br/> <br/>Anna</td>', '*Critique Club* What a silly photo.  As I look at it I have to say it really is good considering all the issues. I''m guessing the spots in the photo were raindrops on your lens.  Your focus isn''t to bad and you have a very cute idea here, the umbrella adds the humor to the photo.  Only real attention getter in this photo is that white bag it seems overly exposed. It does meet the challenge well. Anna', '2003-07-22 12:20:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(363479, 27805, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil<br/> <br/>Composition - Nicely framed abstract is my first thought on this shot.  I''m not overwhelmed by its topic appropriateness, but I think it works for this okay.  Obviously its at night, so that''s enough for me to say okay for this challenge.  I like the rays coming from the explosion, especially the short ones.  What I''d really like to see is some color, but if it isn''t there, it isn''t there.  I like the way you framed this and the way it fills the frame.  I think with a single shot like this, you almost have to frame it similar to this.  Unfortunately this is not a real compelling picture to me.  That''s where the lack of color hurts the most.  It''s a fine capture regardless.  But it doesn''t have a real holding power to keep the viewers right there looking at every little aspect.<br/> <br/>Technically - Your timing was impeccable.  Good exposure.  I think bumping the shutter down to 1.3" or 1.6" would''ve given you a lot of the same effect with a little more color saturation, if there was any.  If there wasn''t color, then it doesn''t make much difference.  This is a photo that actually would be artistically stronger if you messed with it some.  Not really for the challenge so much, but play with color balance or colorize it and then do some random curves adjustments just to see what happens.  It is a cool pattern, so you have a good basis from which to start.<br/> <br/>Overall - Not a bad picture, but not a strong one either.  I think your score is a little lower than I would expect for this challenge.  Some of that might have to do with the frequency the voters saw this particular subject.  But what can you do?  What I think it needs is something to keep the viewers hooked for a longer period of time.  Good luck in the future and take care - Bob</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil Composition - Nicely framed abstract is my first thought on this shot.  I''m not overwhelmed by its topic appropriateness, but I think it works for this okay.  Obviously its at night, so that''s enough for me to say okay for this challenge.  I like the rays coming from the explosion, especially the short ones.  What I''d really like to see is some color, but if it isn''t there, it isn''t there.  I like the way you framed this and the way it fills the frame.  I think with a single shot like this, you almost have to frame it similar to this.  Unfortunately this is not a real compelling picture to me.  That''s where the lack of color hurts the most.  It''s a fine capture regardless.  But it doesn''t have a real holding power to keep the viewers right there looking at every little aspect. Technically - Your timing was impeccable.  Good exposure.  I think bumping the shutter down to 1.3" or 1.6" would''ve given you a lot of the same effect with a little more color saturation, if there was any.  If there wasn''t color, then it doesn''t make much difference.  This is a photo that actually would be artistically stronger if you messed with it some.  Not really for the challenge so much, but play with color balance or colorize it and then do some random curves adjustments just to see what happens.  It is a cool pattern, so you have a good basis from which to start. Overall - Not a bad picture, but not a strong one either.  I think your score is a little lower than I would expect for this challenge.  Some of that might have to do with the frequency the voters saw this particular subject.  But what can you do?  What I think it needs is something to keep the viewers hooked for a longer period of time.  Good luck in the future and take care - Bob', '2003-07-22 12:32:07.000000', NULL, 0),
